# `actuarial::life_contingencies::hazard_qx`

Cumulative force of mortality from death probabilities.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series actuarial::life_contingencies::hazard_qx(const pluss::Series &qx);
```

## What It Does

Returns {math}`H_k=\sum_{j\le k}\mu_j` with {math}`\mu_j=-\ln(1-q_j)`: the running sum of the one-year forces, the same length as `qx`. The survival probability to the end of year `k + 1` is {math}`e^{-H_k}`, so this is the **cumulative hazard**; it is the function to plot or compare when mortality is described as a hazard. [`survival_force`](survival_force.md) goes the other way from forces to the curve.

## Parameters

`qx`
: One-dimensional series of probabilities in {math}`[0,1)`.

## Returns

A non-decreasing series of the same length as `qx`.

## Errors

Throws `force_qx: qx must lie in [0,1)` for a probability of one or more.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: cumulative hazard

Five years of probabilities.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  out.writeln(life::hazard_qx(qx).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: the survival from the hazard

`exp(-H)` is the survival at the end of each year.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  out.writeln("exp(-H)   =", life::hazard_qx(qx).neg().exp().items<double>());
  out.writeln("survival  =", actuarial::mortality::from_qx(qx).survival().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: the last value is the total force

It equals the sum of the forces.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  out.writeln(life::hazard_qx(qx).items<double>().back(), life::force_qx(qx).sum().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: a rising hazard

The hazard of a Gompertz law grows faster than linearly.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series ages = pluss::make_series({60.0, 61.0, 62.0, 63.0});
  out.writeln(life::hazard_qx(life::gompertz_qx(ages, 0.0001, 1.08)).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the error

A certain death makes the hazard infinite and is refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)life::hazard_qx(pluss::make_series({0.1, 1.0})); });
}
```

